  "principleMovementQualityTitle": "Movement Quality > Movement Quantity",
  "principleMovementQualityFact": "For EDS/HSD, low-load exercise focused on motor control reduces pain and improves function more reliably than high-volume training.",
  "principleMovementQualityInspiration": "Your joints don't need endless reps — they need mindful, precise control. Training proprioception is what stabilizes loose joints, not pushing them to their limit.",

  "principleHealingClockTitle": "Respecting the Biological Healing Clock",
  "principleHealingClockFact": "Tendons, ligaments, and joint capsules have limited blood supply and heal far slower than muscle — EDS extends this further.",
  "principleHealingClockInspiration": "\"Start low, go slow\" isn't a lack of progress — it's a clinical necessity that lets your nervous system rebuild stabilizing muscle without overstressing fragile ligaments.",

  "principleDelayedSorenessTitle": "Validating Delayed Soreness (DOMS)",
  "principleDelayedSorenessFact": "Hypermobile individuals experience significantly greater, more intense delayed-onset muscle soreness than non-hypermobile people after eccentric exercise.",
  "principleDelayedSorenessInspiration": "Soreness days later doesn't mean you're broken — your muscles are working harder to stabilize loose joints. Extra recovery time is biology, not a failure of will.",

  "principleAutoEscalationTitle": "Rejecting the Auto-Escalation Trap",
  "principleAutoEscalationFact": "Commercial trackers that auto-raise goals after a big day can push chronic pain / dysautonomia patients past what their nervous system needs for recovery.",
  "principleAutoEscalationInspiration": "Rest isn't a missed day. Your capacity is a range that shifts daily with sleep, hydration, and pain — not an arbitrary target that only ever goes up. This is why this app's baseline excludes rest and flare days instead of averaging them in.",

  "principleCinderellaMusclesTitle": "Rescuing the 'Cinderella Muscles'",
  "principleCinderellaMusclesFact": "Lax joints force stabilizing muscles into constant low-level contraction (the 'Cinderella hypothesis'), causing metabolic overload and trigger points.",
  "principleCinderellaMusclesInspiration": "Your stabilizing muscles work a 24-hour shift just to hold you upright. Frequent gentle micro-breaks — twists, isometric holds — restore blood flow and break the pain cycle.",

  "principleStiffnessParadoxTitle": "The Stiffness Paradox",
  "principleStiffnessParadoxFact": "Fascia can thicken (densify) to compensate for lax joints, creating stiffness despite loose ligaments — aggressive stretching can worsen instability.",
  "principleStiffnessParadoxInspiration": "Tightness is often your body protecting your joints. Gentle fascial gliding and core stabilization reassure your nervous system more safely than a deep stretch.",

  "principleEccentricWorkTitle": "Eccentric Work: More Strength for Fewer Spoons",
  "principleEccentricWorkFact": "Eccentric (lengthening) contractions generate high force at a lower metabolic and cardiovascular cost than concentric (lifting) contractions of the same intensity — and unaccustomed muscle damage is largely preventable through gradual, low-intensity progression.",
  "principleEccentricWorkInspiration": "This is why 'Eccentric' work is worth tagging separately here — it's a way to build real strength without spending spoons you don't have, especially useful if dysautonomia limits how much cardiovascular effort you can spare on a given day.",

  "principleRepeatedBoutEffectTitle": "Soreness Fades With Practice — the Repeated Bout Effect",
  "principleRepeatedBoutEffectFact": "After an initial bout of eccentric exercise, the same movement causes markedly less soreness and muscle damage the next time — protection that can last weeks to months.",
  "principleRepeatedBoutEffectInspiration": "If a new eccentric exercise leaves you sore the first couple of times, that's expected and it's not a sign to stop — it typically eases on its own as your muscles adapt, faster than you might expect.",

  "principleMuscleDamageCaveatTitle": "An Important Caveat: Muscle Damage ≠ Joint Safety",
  "principleMuscleDamageCaveatFact": "\"Muscle damage is usually fine\" research above was done in general and older-adult populations, not specifically in hypermobile connective tissue. That is not the same claim as \"joint and capsule loading is fine\" for EDS/HSD bodies.",
  "principleMuscleDamageCaveatInspiration": "Treat muscle soreness and joint/capsule signals as two separate things worth tracking separately — easing muscle soreness is a good sign, but pain, instability, or swelling around a joint is its own signal and deserves its own caution, regardless of what the muscle soreness is doing.",

  "restFactNoBehind": "There is no 'behind' in a chronic condition. The only comparison that matters is you-today to you-yesterday, gently.",
  "restFactEveryEntryCounts": "Every log entry — even 'I rested' or 'I couldn't log anything' — is useful data for you and your care team. It all counts.",
